i think life is really tough for working  parents but especially working mums who  are bearing the brunt of it and  especially people with  kids under 10 what we're hearing is that  even with kids going back to school  nurseries are still struggling some are  putting caps on numbers  uh breakfast clubs for older children  after school  clubs aren't necessarily up and running  and of course that  whole network that working parents rely  on  of mates and grandparents and child  minders  is under real pressure so i think there  are an awful lot of  working mums out there who are feeling  really stressed right now  it's interesting isn't it because i  guess lots of people think well  nurseries are reopened schools are  reopened what's the problem but it's  almost a real  kind of patchwork of support that so  many working parents  rely on whether it's grandparents or  after school clubs  that simply isn't there at the minute  well you pull one thread and the worry  is that  everything unravels and what we're  seeing is  you know even where people can work at  home it's very often the case that it's  uh the working mother who's taking on  the  big responsibility for children and  having kids around your feet and trying  to do a job isn't easy  and some of the choices that women are  being forced to make  include cutting their hours of work or  going in really early or ending up doing  effectively an evening shift to try and  keep on top of their jobs so  i think a lot of working women in  particular are desperate for some more  flexibility  but also desperate to see the government  take child care seriously  and provide some support especially for  the nursery sector  it's interesting to hear you talk though  about how working  mothers in particular are bearing the  brunt of course you know lots of dads  are spending lots of time  uh with child care responsibilities too  but studies by the  institute for fiscal studies and others  have seemed to suggest it's mainly women  picking up  quite a lot of the burden what does this  mean for women's  job prospects well i i think the risk  here is that we see  attitudes and behaviors setting women  back decades you know we've made a lot  of progress  in terms of equal pay respect for women  a recognition that you know women want  to get on at work  as much as men but when women are  picking up  the lion's share of the burden not just  of childcare but it looks like housework  too  that makes life difficult and it it's  exposed just how fragile  and underfunded our child care system is  and  you know i i think the government needs  to understand  that we need working patterns that work  for parents and especially working moms  as much as anybody else there's got to  be two-way flexibility we'd like to see  a right  to flexible working from day one  and you talk there a little bit about  this but what do you think the  government needs to do  right now to try and help working  parents get back to the office as it  says it wants to  well all our work has shown that we've  still got lots of nurseries  that are on the brink of collapse many  have had no choice but to cap numbers  and that questions whether they're  financially viable  over this coronavirus period and as i  said before  a lot of the informal care that working  parents rely on  has unraveled child minders families you  know it's much  more difficult so i think we need you  know  if anybody's going to get a bail out the  child care sector deserves a bear out  because that's crucial for the economy  that  allows working parents to do their job  so it would be a  force economy not to give proper support  to childcare  but i think we also need to see progress  on two other fronts you know there needs  to be that right to flexible working  where we can make it work with the  business let's have two-way flexibility  because i can tell you as you probably  know all too well  that when you're doing long hours and a  precious job  having that bit more flexibility can  make all the difference so let's have  that right to flexible working and a bit  more compassion  on that front but i also think we've got  to make progress on equal pay  uh you know we're not there yet we've  still got a way to go  before women get equal pay for work of  equal value  and as long as there's unequal pay then  it feels like women are always going to  end up picking up the pieces when  there's a child's care problem  because in the end they're not often  on average earning as much as their  partner so we've got to see action on  that front too
 

